"(defn f [vm v] (let [box {:s (:store vm)}] (assoc (:s box) 'x v)))\"\ntest/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj:498:   \"(defn f [vm v] (apply assoc (:store vm) ['x v]))\"\ntest/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj:499:   \"(defn f [vm v] (swap! (atom (:store vm)) assoc 'x v))\"\ntest/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj:500:   \"(defn f [vms v] (when-first [h (map :store vms)] (assoc h 'x v)))\"\ntest/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj:501:   \"(defn f [vm v] ((partial assoc (:store vm)) 'x v))\"\ntest/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj:502:   \"(defn f [vm v] ((comp #(assoc % 'x v) :store) vm))\"\ntest/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj:503:   \"(defn f [vm v] (let [w assoc] (w (:store vm) 'x v)))\"])\nf94649e3 fix: allowlist PHP staging byte-store context\n ...write-allowlist.codex-gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++++\n test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj             |  4 ++++\n 2 files changed, 31 insertions(+)\nCodex session 01a1f98e-f29f-79c1-aa87-658a859b1f7d\nCodex thread 01a1f98e-f29f-79c1-aa87-658a859b1f7d\nls: cannot access '.clojuredart': No such file or directory\nls: cannot access 'test/cljd-out': No such file or directory\nls: cannot access 'lib/cljd-out': No such file or directory\n-rw-rw-r--  1 sto sto   74 Nov 20 10:23 .git\ndrwxrwxr-x  3 sto sto 4096 Nov 20 10:23 build\ndrwxrwxr-x 50 sto sto 4096 Nov 20 10:23 node_modules\ndrwxrwxr-x  3 sto sto 4096 Nov 20 10:23 target\n","exit_code":2,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '244,372p;541,575p;638,683p;880,1038p' docs/agents/build-n-test.md && sed -n '230,370p;460,515p' docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '244,372p;541,575p;638,683p;880,1038p' docs/agents/build-n-test.md && sed -n '230,370p;460,515p' docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md\"","aggregated_output":"### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n   **Failure-reproduction rule (owner ruling 2026-11-01): when a test fails anywhere — a\n   gate, the nightly, or a delegate report — reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the\n   failing test, never the whole suite.** JVM: `clojure -M:test -n <ns>` (a single\n   deftest: add `-v <ns>/<var-name>`). Node: `node target/node-tests.js --test=<ns>`\n   (built by `bb build:yin-repl-node` first only if that test needs the Node REPL).\n   Dart: `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only <ns>[/<test-name>]` (owner ruling\n   2026-11-07: the focus rule runs the failing TEST, not the namespace —\n   `NS/test-name` entries become flutter `--plain-name` filters so only the\n   named deftests execute; fall back to bare `<ns>` only when the failing\n   var inside the namespace is not yet known, and narrow as soon as it is).\n   Fix against the failing test alone, re-run it green, then climb back up\n   (G1 for the home subsystem, G2 for dependents).\n   Dart bug-chases may set `DATOM_FAIL_FAST=1` (owner ruling 2026-11-07): the\n   scoped run stops at the first failure so the report arrives in minutes instead\n   of after the full scoped set. Gate runs never set it — the merge bar needs the\n   complete pass/fail picture. All dart runs use `--reporter expanded`, which\n   streams failure names and positions inline so an interrupted run still retains\n   them (the nightly's compact reporter lost 7 failure names to its 30000s wall,\n   2026-11-06).\n   Merge bar for a fix branch (owner ruling 2026-11-07, sharpening the 2026-11-01\n   ruling): when the work is fixing already-failing tests, the previously-failing\n   tests re-run green IS the merge bar — a fix branch merges on that alone; the\n   G1/G2 climb applies to new feature slices, not to fixes. The full suite still\n   gets its end-to-end confirmation from the daily nightly (04:00 VN), which is\n   what catches collateral damage, not a pre-merge suite run.\n   Never reproduce or chase a failure with the full suite or the slow tier — the full\n   suite is not a debugging tool, and running it locally for one failing test is the\n   exact misuse that drove a laptop load average over 100 (2026-11-01). A failure that\n   only reproduces under the full suite's concurrency is diagnosed with the suite's own\n   logs (its per-shard/per-test output), not by re-running the suite repeatedly.\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master (merged in place by the 2026-11-01 owner ruling)**: The landing gate\n   for a change is the **tests that cover the feature being built** — `bb test:changed`\n   (G2) already includes the reverse-require closure of the changed files, and a wide\n   change still routes to the full quick lane. The complete suite is no longer a\n   per-merge gate: it runs twice a day on the remote hosts (see \"Cadence\" below), so a\n   break is caught within hours without charging every merge ~400 minutes of slow tier.\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge\n   (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n   (owner decision 2026-10-12), and implicitly twice daily by the scheduled run below.\n   G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n### Gate log hygiene: per-run directories, never shared per-lane logs (2026-11-14 incident)\n\n`bb test:sub` writes its per-lane detail logs to `target/profile/sub-clj.log`,\n`sub-cljs.log`, `sub-cljd.log` inside the checkout — and **each invocation\noverwrites them**. A gate script that runs several suites back-to-back while\nonly appending the summary stream to a shared out file loses every lane's\nper-fixture detail the moment the next suite starts. Measured 2026-11-14\n(yang.php branch-tip gate, jupiter): the yang.php lane failed with\n`yangphp-rc=1`; the dao.stream precondition that ran immediately after\noverwrote `sub-clj.log`, and the failure detail was unrecoverable — forcing a\nfull lane rerun (~2 h).\n\n**Rule: one run, one directory.** Each remote gate run gets its own\ndirectory (per gate, per attempt), and every lane's log is copied there\nbefore the next suite starts:\n\n```sh\n# per-gate run dir; the summaries live beside the detail logs\nRUN=~/ci/logs/<gate-name>-\\$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)\nmkdir -p \"\\$RUN\"\nbb test:sub yang.php > \"\\$RUN\"/yang-php.out 2>&1\ncp target/profile/sub-*.log \"\\$RUN\"/          # bank BEFORE the next suite\necho \"yangphp-rc=\\$?\" >> \"\\$RUN\"/summary.txt\nbb test:sub dao.stream > \"\\$RUN\"/dao-stream.out 2>&1\ncp target/profile/sub-*.log \"\\$RUN\"/\necho \"daostream-rc=\\$?\" >> \"\\$RUN\"/summary.txt\n```\n\nAlternatively use a fresh `git worktree` (or clone) per gate when the\ncompile caches make reuse expensive — the point is that no later\ninvocation can overwrite an earlier lane's evidence. The failure summary\n(counts, rc) survives in the shared out file; the per-fixture detail\nsurvives only in the run directory.\n\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). The laptop additionally never runs the slow tier or the nightly\ndriver (see the sharded-lane section) — a full slow tier there reached a load\naverage over 100 on 2026-11-01; the remote hosts own that tier. On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\n\nOwner, verbatim: \"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\nchanges\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \"it should not be for\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\"\n\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\n\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on\n  shiva and jupiter, hourly cron plus a 04:00 Vietnam guard and flock,\n  documented under \"Cadence\" above; the driver fetches and checks out\n  origin/master before anything runs). Every lane, across the host pair:\n  the JVM full suite on jupiter, the full Node and Dart suites on shiva.\n  Its results are a\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\n\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\nnightly owns the follow-up. A gate claim names its tier: fast\n`bb test:sub:clj <sub>` excludes `^:slow` tests, so \"0 failures\" from it\nnever asserts the slow tier ran — when the diff touches slow-tagged tests\n(G2's `--slow` clause), the claim cites the slow-tier or per-test linked\nruns separately (2026-11-17 lesson: three include/const gate claims read\n\"green\" while the slow linked rows were only proven by separate fresh-JVM\nruns).\n\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling, restored 2026-10-31 —\na dedup pass dropped this section along with a stale duplicate of the\ntwo-tier policy; both stand):** before any merge into master, the\n`dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is what everything is\nbuilt on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the subsystem's tests,\n`--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded surfaces) is part of every\nbranch gate regardless of which subsystems the diff's own changes\nselect, because a change can break the foundation without touching it.\nA red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or diagnosed as\npre-existing by the owning seat.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores, and measure before relying on a speedup. (Historical record — the\n\"free laptop\" comparison predates the 2026-11-04 ruling that suite-scale runs\nbelong on the remote hosts.)\n\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\n\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\nlaptop — route Node lanes to shiva; the laptop comparison is historical and\nsuite-scale Node runs no longer belong there per the 2026-11-04 ruling);\n**Dart quick tier\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\n\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\n\"Patch does not apply\" or \"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\".\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\n\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\n\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \"run tests remotely\nover SSH\" to \"delegate whole work agents remotely\": a heavy implementation or\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\n\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\nreference):\n\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\n   prepare the scratch clone per \"Get the code there\".\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \"$(mise env -s\n   bash)\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\n   inside the same command.\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\n\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n\n```clojure\n(segment-matches? address payload)\n```\n\nIt:\n\n1. parses the address;\n2. obtains `canonical-bytes` for the payload;\n3. selects the algorithm carried by the address;\n4. hashes the bytes;\n5. compares the digest; and\n6. confirms that reformatting the parsed pair reproduces the canonical address.\n\nThis dispatch is the core multihash behavior. A store may contain an explicitly minted SHA-256 object and a default-minted BLAKE3 object at the same time, and each verifies under its own algorithm.\n\nMalformed, unsupported, noncanonical, or mismatched addresses return false. Canonical-encoder refusal also returns false, making `segment-matches?` a total verification predicate. Minting operations such as `materialize!` and `segment-key` continue to throw on encoder refusal.\n\nOutside `dao.jing`, this is the normal operation for validating an address/payload pair. Callers do not manually compare `segment-hash` with `content-hash`, nor re-mint under the default with `segment-key`.\n\n### `materialize!`\n\nRetain:\n\n```clojure\n(materialize! handle payload)\n```\n\nAdd:\n\n```clojure\n(materialize! handle payload {:algorithm :sha256})\n```\n\nThe one-argument form mints with BLAKE3. The explicit form supports any registered algorithm.\n\nOn `:present`, verify the stored payload with:\n\n```clojure\n(segment-matches? address stored)\n```\n\nDo not use the default algorithm to validate it.\n\nWhen copying content already named by an address into another store, derive the minting algorithm from that address:\n\n```clojure\n(materialize! destination payload\n  {:algorithm (segment-algorithm address)})\n```\n\nThe returned address must equal the source address. This is required for ordinary multi-algorithm operation: a SHA-256 object fetched from a peer remains a SHA-256 object when cached, even though BLAKE3 is the default for unrelated new content.\n\n### `get`\n\nThe signature remains:\n\n```clojure\n(get handle address not-found)\n```\n\nIt accepts every address recognized by `segment-address?`, including both initial algorithms. It rejects arbitrary keys before consulting the backend.\n\n`get` need not rehash every successful read. Existing backend insertion and replay checks, plus higher-level verified readers, retain their responsibilities. A consumer that verifies fetched content uses `segment-matches?`.\n\nHistorical, registry era: the paragraph above describes the registry-era\nread policy, written before the canonical CBOR swap. The current rule in\n`dao.jing.cbor.md` is stricter: `get` hash-verifies every read against the\nrequested address before decoding.\n\n### File-backend codec round-trip (historical, registry era)\n\nThis section describes the registry-era printer codec and its round-trip\ncheck, both removed by the canonical CBOR swap. File replay now performs\nstrict CBOR validation of each frame and payload; see `dao.jing.cbor.md`\n(*Memory and files*).\n\n`dao.jing.file/validate-codec-round-trip!` is not an address/payload validator. It compares a payload's identity with its own `pr-str`/EDN round trip to ensure that the file codec does not discard address-significant information.\n\nIt does not use `segment-matches?`. Both sides must be hashed with the algorithm selected for the pending address:\n\n```clojure\n(content-hash payload  {:algorithm algorithm})\n(content-hash replayed {:algorithm algorithm})\n```\n\nThis makes explicit SHA-256 materialization and default BLAKE3 materialization obey the same file-codec rule.\n\n## Clean introduction\n\nThe registry is introduced without a migration phase.\n\nThere are no deployed DaoJing stores or published addresses to preserve. The implementation therefore changes the contract atomically:\n\n- BLAKE3 becomes the one-argument minting default immediately.\n- Explicit SHA-256 minting is available immediately as an ordinary registry operation.\n- Existing development fixtures are regenerated.\n- Existing file stores are not upgraded or read through a compatibility path.\n- Existing checkpoint shapes are replaced rather than dual-read.\n- All peers in a development composition run the new address contract together.\n- No old-address alias, graph migration, dual checkpoint schema, or format negotiation is added.\n\nThe absence of migration machinery does not weaken multi-algorithm support. SHA-256 and BLAKE3 coexist because callers may intentionally select either after the registry lands, not because content from an earlier system is being carried forward.\n\n## Call-site audit and required dispositions\n\nHash use outside `dao.jing` falls into four classes:\n\n1. frozen non-Jing hash contracts;\n2. minting new content;\n3. validating content against an existing address; and\n4. copying addressed content while preserving its selected algorithm.\n\nThe classification is a maintained design artifact. Minting and verification must not be conflated.\n\n### Explicit hash contracts\n\nThe six de Bruijn consumers and the DHT routing hash are independent of\nDaoJing's minting default. They have their own encoders and contracts.\n\nThe six surviving de Bruijn hash sites use explicit BLAKE3 after the\nphase-8ab regeneration (2026-10-14): stack and register descriptor hashes,\nH and R checksums, and the projection dimension and node hashes. Their\nencoders remain independent of DaoJing's addressing encoder and minting\ndefault. A is the sole code identity; H and R are derived checksums.\n\nThe DHT node ID remains SHA-256 because it defines the DHT routing\nidentity space. Changing it is a separate DHT protocol decision.\n\n### Primitive-profile identifier\n\n`yin.vm/primitive-profile` constructs:\n\n```clojure\n:yin.k.pp/blake3-<digest of (content-hash description {:algorithm :blake3})>\n```\n\nThe profile digest is computed explicitly with BLAKE3 (owner decision 2026-10-12, with the capability seal and the owner tag), not by riding the default. The `blake3-` label stays truthful even if the default algorithm later changes. This is an explicit VM contract using a first-class registry algorithm, not a compatibility fallback.\n\nA future canonical-encoding clean break may change this digest because `content-hash` follows the one current canonical encoder. If primitive-profile identity must remain frozen across that break, its owning VM contract must first pin its own byte encoder rather than relying on DaoJing's changing content encoder.\n\n\n- H or R, checking its derived backend-format bytes; and\n- a DaoJing segment address, identifying the stored Jing value under its selected registry algorithm.\n\nThe two identities must not be conflated merely because both can use the same algorithm.\n\n## Host libraries\n\nUse the portability spike's validated BLAKE3 providers:\n\n- JVM: `io.github.rctcwyvrn/blake3` 1.3\n- ClojureScript/Node: `@noble/hashes` 2.4.0\n- ClojureDart: `blake3_dart` 1.0.0\n\nPin exact versions. A content-identity dependency must not use floating ranges.\n\nThe portability spike's cross-provider result left no durable repository artifact. The implementation therefore commits official-vector fixtures and the cross-provider digest table as the evidence of record.\n\nSHA-256 keeps its current per-host implementations, including the hand-written ClojureDart implementation. That code serves the first-class SHA-256 registry entry and the explicit DHT contract.\n\n### Maintenance risks\n\nThe JVM BLAKE3 dependency is pure bytecode and MIT licensed but stale, with its Maven release dating to 2020. Acceptance requires:\n\n- source and license review;\n- official BLAKE3 known-answer tests;\n- chunk and tree-boundary vectors;\n- a cross-host fixture corpus;\n- isolation behind `digest-bytes`; and\n- a provider-replacement procedure that requires complete conformance before adoption.\n\nThe Dart dependency is pure Dart and passed official vectors, but has only one release and a short history. It receives the same fixture and replacement discipline. It is also the Dart host's first external cryptographic dependency, so dependency resolution and release-build execution are explicit gates.\n\n`@noble/hashes` is actively maintained, but its ESM/package integration must be exercised in the current Shadow-CLJS test and optimized-release builds.\n\nAny provider version or replacement that changes a pinned digest is rejected as nonconforming to the registered algorithm.\n\n## Sequencing with canonical CBOR\n\nThe algorithm-registry epic landed first, over the order-normalized print\nencoder of the time; the canonical CBOR clean break then replaced that\nencoder on 2026-09-24. Both are complete.\n\nThe registry epic established:\n\n- the flat multihash address grammar;\n- permanent BLAKE3 and SHA-256 entries;\n- BLAKE3 as the minting default;\n- explicit SHA-256 minting;\n- algorithm-directed verification; and\n- algorithm-preserving copy paths.\n\nCanonical CBOR was a separate clean-break epic, now landed. The codec in\n`dao.jing.cbor`, whose byte contract is frozen by\n`test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json`, is the addressing encoder:\n`canonical-bytes` is `dao.jing.cbor/encode`. The algorithm registry was\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,120p' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/eval_coordinator.cljc && sed -n '175,205p;240,287p' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/siteset.cljc && sed -n '130,185p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/interpreter.cljc && sed -n '245,280p' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc && sed -n '288,359p' test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj && rg -n 'defn (assoc-module|register-host-module)|defn-? (parse-segment-address|segment-key)|cljd-agg|node-tests|test:sub' src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn && ps -u \\\"\"'$(id -u)\" -o pid,args | rg '\"'cljd_agg|cljd.runner|flutter.*test|shadow.cljs'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,120p' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/eval_coordinator.cljc && sed -n '175,205p;240,287p' src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/siteset.cljc && sed -n '130,185p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/interpreter.cljc && sed -n '245,280p' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc && sed -n '288,359p' test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj && rg -n 'defn (assoc-module|register-host-module)|defn-? (parse-segment-address|segment-key)|cljd-agg|node-tests|test:sub' src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn && ps -u \\\"\"'$(id -u)\" -o pid,args | rg '\"'cljd_agg|cljd.runner|flutter.*test|shadow.cljs'\"","aggregated_output":"(ns yang.php.antlr.eval-coordinator\n  \"Composition-supplied eval frontend and stream service.\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [yang.antlr.packet :as packet]\n            [yang.php.antlr.cst :as cst]\n            [yang.php.antlr.eval-admission :as admission]\n            [yang.php.antlr.eval-publication :as publication]\n            [yang.php.antlr.eval-transport :as transport]\n            [yang.php.antlr.scope :as scope]\n            [yang.php.antlr.siteset :as siteset]))\n\n\n(defn make-worker\n  []\n  (cst/make-eval-worker))\n\n\n(defn compile-request\n  \"Prepare one request for later eval-unit emission. Identity includes the\n   complete request (including bytes and caller-supplied revisions/options),\n   the source goal, grammar and admission profile. No caller runtime state is\n   needed by this service.\"\n  [worker request]\n  (let [{:keys [source] :as admitted} (admission/admit-request request)]\n    (if-not source\n      admitted\n      (let [pk (cst/parse-source worker (:yang.compile/unit request) source)\n            identity {:request request\n                      :yang.php/source (:yang.php/source request)\n                      :yang.php.eval/profile-revision admission/profile-revision\n                      :yang.php/source-goal :eval\n                      :yang.php/grammar cst/grammar-id}]\n        (if (:yang.php/not-php pk)\n          {:request request :identity identity :cst pk}\n          (let [validated (packet/validate! pk {:source source})]\n            (cond-> {:request request :identity identity :cst validated}\n              (= :yang.cst/ok (:yang.cst/outcome validated))\n              (assoc :scope (scope/analyze validated)))))))))\n\n\n(defn compile-transport-request\n  \"Validate a sealed transport request before handing it to the parser worker.\n   Refusals include the request identity only when it is portable data.\"\n  [worker request]\n  (if-let [defect (transport/request-defect request)]\n    (cond-> {:yang.compile/outcome :yang.compile/rejected\n             :yang.compile/reason :yang.compile/invalid-request\n             :yang.compile/detail defect}\n      (and (map? request) (contains? request :yang.compile/request)\n           (transport/portable-data? (:yang.compile/request request)))\n      (assoc :yang.compile/request (:yang.compile/request request)))\n    (compile-request worker request)))\n\n\n(defn- compile-and-publish*!\n  \"Compile a sealed request and publish its canonical module and occurrence\n   set to the composition's store. Linker admission is a separate step.\"\n  [{:keys [worker store registry stage-requirement]} request]\n  (let [{:keys [cst identity] :as prepared} (compile-transport-request worker request)\n        correlation (transport/correlation request)]\n    (cond\n      (:yang.compile/outcome prepared) prepared\n      (not= :yang.cst/ok (:yang.cst/outcome cst))\n      (transport/rejected request :yang.compile/invalid-source\n                          {:yang.compile/diagnostics (:yang.cst/errors cst)})\n      :else\n      (let [name (symbol (str \"eval.\" (name (jing/segment-key identity))))\n            result (publication/publish! store registry name prepared)]\n        (if (= :ok (:status result))\n          (let [address (:address result)\n                site (:sites result)\n                stage (when stage-requirement\n                        (siteset/stage registry stage-requirement\n                                       {address [(:record-address site)]}))\n                derived (when stage (stage {:store store :datoms [] :principals []}\n                                           name address))]\n            (if (and derived (not= :ok (:status derived)))\n              (transport/rejected request :yang.compile/derivation-failed\n                                  {:yang.compile/detail derived})\n              (merge correlation {:yang.compile/outcome :yang.compile/published\n                                  :yang.compile/module name\n                                  :yang.compile/address address\n                                  :yang.compile/source-address (:source-address result)\n                                  :yang.compile/site-record (:record-address site)}\n                     (when derived {:yang.compile/active-address (:address derived)\n                                    :yang.compile/derivation (:derivation derived)}))))\n          (transport/rejected request :yang.compile/publication-failed\n                              {:yang.compile/detail (select-keys result [:reason :status])}))))))\n\n\n(defn compile-and-publish!\n  \"Compile and publish, returning classified frontend diagnostics to the caller.\"\n  [state request]\n  (try\n    (compile-and-publish*! state request)\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) e\n      (let [diagnostic (ex-data e)]\n        (if (:yang.php/diagnostic diagnostic)\n          (transport/rejected request\n                              (if (= :yang.php/compile-error\n                                     (:yang.php/diagnostic diagnostic))\n                                :yang.compile/invalid-source\n                                :yang.compile/unsupported)\n                              {:yang.compile/diagnostic diagnostic})\n          (throw e))))))\n\n\n(defn serve\n  \"Serve at most `budget` requests. Advance the immutable request cursor only\n   after its answer is appended, so a full response stream loses no request.\"\n  ([state] (serve state 32))\n  ([state budget]\n   (loop [state state, remaining budget]\n     (if (not (pos? remaining))\n       state\n       (let [read (or (:pending state)\n                      (let [item (stream/next (:requests state) (:cursor state))]\n                        (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome item))\n                          {:cursor (:dao.stream/cursor item)\n                             (= :literal (:type key)) (symbol? (:value key))\n                             (contains? #{:literal :lambda} (:type value)))))]\n    (and (= :application (:type ast))\n         (= :lambda (:type operator))\n         (= :literal (get-in operator [:body :type]))\n         (nil? (get-in operator [:body :value]))\n         (= (count (:params operator)) (count ops))\n         (seq (butlast ops))\n         (every? definition? (butlast ops))\n         (= (core-module/require-node 'php) (dissoc (last ops) :tail?)))))\n\n\n(defn- derive-closure\n  [ctx registry requirement records manifest derived]\n  (let [handle (:store ctx)\n        runtime (or (:runtime requirement) '#{php php.sp yang.call})\n        candidates (into (vec (get records manifest))\n                         (discover (:datoms ctx) (:principals ctx) manifest))\n        admitted (stage-admit handle manifest candidates)]\n    (if (not= :ok (:status admitted)) admitted\n        (let [m (read-at handle manifest)\n              deps (sort-by (comp str key)\n                            (remove (comp runtime key) (:yin.module/requires m)))\n              below (reduce (fn [acc [name address]]\n                              (if (contains? (:derived acc) address) acc\n                                  (let [r (derive-closure ctx registry requirement records\n                                                          address (:derived acc))]\n                                    (if (= :ok (:status r))\n                                      (assoc acc :derived (:derived r))\n                                      (reduced (cond-> r\n                                                 (nil? (:name r)) (assoc :name name)\n                                                  :requires requires, :primitives primitives}))]\n                      (if (or (nil? out) (= :refused (:status out)))\n                        (stage-refusal :yang.php.antlr/instrumentation-failed manifest)\n                        (let [record (assoc (:record d) :yin.ledger/output\n                                            (:yin.module/tree (:manifest out)))]\n                          {:status :ok, :address (:address out),\n                           :derivation (jing/materialize! handle record),\n                           :derived (assoc (:derived below) manifest (:address out))})))\n                    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) e\n                      (assoc (stage-refusal :yang.php.antlr/instrumentation-failed manifest)\n                             :message (ex-message e)))))))))))\n\n\n(defn stage\n  \"A write-buffered, bottom-up stage over verified PHP site publications.\"\n  [registry requirement records]\n  (let [runtime (or (:runtime requirement) '#{php php.sp yang.call})]\n    (fn [ctx name address]\n      (cond\n        (contains? runtime name) {:status :ok, :address address}\n        (:noninterruptible requirement) {:status :ok, :address address}\n        (not (:hooks requirement)) (stage-refusal :yang.php.antlr/profile-undeclared address)\n        :else\n        (let [{:keys [handle flush!]} (overlay (:store ctx))\n              result (derive-closure (assoc ctx :store handle) registry requirement\n                                     records address {})]\n          (if (= :ok (:status result))\n            (do (flush!) (select-keys result [:status :address :derivation]))\n            result))))))\n              :address address\n              :recorded (:kind status)}}\n\n      (= :loading (:status status))\n      {:node node, :body pair/pending-body, :waits address}\n\n      (= :failed (:status status)) {:node (dht/forget node address)\n                                    :body (linker.dht/load-refusal status)}\n\n      :else\n      (let [bindings (linker.dht/dependency-bindings node authority address)\n            unbound (if (map? bindings)\n                      bindings\n                      (some (fn [b]\n                              (when-not (= :ok (:binding b))\n                                (merge {:status :refused\n                                        :reason :yin.link.dht/dependency-binding}\n                                       b)))\n                            bindings))]\n        {:node node\n         :body (or unbound (dht-link node request address))}))))\n\n\n(defn- dht-context\n  \"The stage context of a DHT source over `node`: its store, every\n   loaded snapshot's datoms and the declared principals.\"\n  [source node]\n  {:store (dht/store node)\n   :datoms (into []\n                 (mapcat #(linker.dht/snapshot-datoms node %))\n                 (linker.dht/snapshots node))\n   :principals (:principals source)})\n\n\n(defn- dht-staged\n  \"`dht-attempt` of the resolved `address` through the composition's stage,\n   a second staged load.  The module itself is phase one: loaded, its\n   dependency bindings checked and linked under the composition's policy.\n   Only an `:ok` module is staged, its refusal or pending state standing\n   as it is.  Phase two is the stage load (`yin.vm.linker.dht/load-stage`,\n   keyed per address): the stage reads the node's store and reports what\n   it lacks, `step` fetches it, and the load is pending on its key\n   meanwhile.  Its context, the loaded snapshot set's datoms and the\n   declared principals, is captured when the load starts; a snapshot\n   change while it is pending does not re-resolve.  A `:failed` load is\n   refused and forgotten, so a later require starts over; a `:loaded`\n   decision that refuses is forgotten likewise (a later index load may\n   change it), one that is `:ok` stays.  A substituted address is then\n   attempted in the module's place and its `:ok` response annotated.\"\n  [source node declared request address]\n  (let [first-try (dht-attempt node declared request address)]\n    (if (or (nil? (:stage source))\n            (not= :ok (:status (:body first-try))))\n      first-try\n      (let [node (:node first-try)\n            key (linker.dht/stage-key address)\n   digest length from the registry entry and lowercase-hex text, and\n   reconstructs the canonical address to reject alternative spellings.\n   Returns {:algorithm :<algo>, :digest \\\"<hex>\\\", :canonical :segment/...}\n   or nil on any rejection (never throws).\"\n  [address]\n  (when (keyword? address)\n    (when (= \"segment\" (namespace address))\n      (let [n (name address)\n            i (str/index-of n \"-\")]\n        (when (and i (pos? i))\n          (let [algo-id (subs n 0 i)\n                digest (subs n (inc i))]\n            (when (re-matches algorithm-id-pattern algo-id)\n              (when-let [algo (clojure.core/get registry-by-address-id algo-id)]\n                (let [{:keys [digest-bytes]}\n                      (clojure.core/get registry algo)]\n                  (when (and (= (* 2 digest-bytes) (count digest))\n                             (re-matches hex-digit-pattern digest))\n                    (let [canonical (keyword \"segment\"\n                                             (str algo-id \"-\" digest))]\n                      (when (= canonical address)\n                        {:algorithm algo,\n                         :digest digest,\n                         :canonical canonical}))))))))))))\n\n\n(defn segment-address?\n  \"True when x is a valid content address in the closed registry:\n   :segment/<algorithm>-<digest-hex>.\"\n  [x]\n  (boolean (parse-segment-address x)))\n\n\n(defn segment-algorithm\n  \"The algorithm keyword (:blake3, :sha256) carried by a segment address.\n   Throws on invalid input.\"\n                  (when (seq sites) [f sites]))))\n        (source-files)))\n\n\n(def allowlist\n  \"file -> {[top-level-name head] count}, with the reason each may write.\"\n  {;; `:store` here is a write-buffering overlay handle over the content\n   ;; store, not a mutable VM store\n   \"src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc\"\n   {[\"stage\" \"assoc\"] 1}\n   ;; PHP staging likewise substitutes a buffered dao.jing byte-store\n   ;; handle in its context; this is not a VM program store write.\n   \"src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/siteset.cljc\"\n   {[\"stage\" \"assoc\"] 1}\n   \"src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\"\n   {;; the one program write, and `put-active`, which routes every program\n    ;; write -- definitions, direct store instructions, the :vm/store-put\n    ;; effect -- to the task's store or the running module's store, both\n    ;; through store-put\n    [\"store-put\" \"assoc\"] 1\n    [\"put-active\" \"assoc\"] 1\n    [\"put-active\" \"store-put\"] 2\n    ;; step 5b's receiver: the live store read, never written. Stream\n    ;; handles, cursor cells, and their advances are written to the private\n    ;; :resources table, never the store (yin.vm.linker.md 7.3, r8).\n    [\"discharge-defect\" :map] 1}\n   ;; the constructor merge (checked); every kernel's program writes --\n   ;; definitions and direct store instructions -- go through\n   ;; engine/put-active\n   \"src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc\"\n   {[\"create-vm\" :map] 1\n    [\"create-vm\" \"merge\"] 1}\n   \"src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc\"\n   {[\"create-vm\" :map] 1\n    [\"create-vm\" \"merge\"] 1}\n   ;; construction: the FFI pair under minted keyword keys\n   \"src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc\"\n   {[\"empty-state\" :map] 1}\n   ;; the waitset library's result carries the store its :advance wrote\n   \"src/cljc/dao/stream/waitset.cljc\"\n   {[\"check\" :map] 1}\n   ;; a dao.space published-index handle, not a VM store; and the two\n   ;; datomic-compat published-read sites (the query value the reader\n   ;; returns, and the checked-source realization), whose payloads name\n   ;; dao.space.query/published reads, never a VM store. Allowlisted by\n   ;; the yang.php orchestrator seat 2026-10-26 to unblock a red master;\n   ;; the sites' owner (datomic-compat track) should amend these reasons.\n   \"src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc\"\n   {[\"open-published!\" :map] 1\n    [\"published-db\" :map] 1\n    [\"realize-checked-st\" :map] 1}\n   ;; the REPL history keys *1 *2 *3 (fixed symbols, none reserved), and\n   ;; the expander consumer's macro store\n   \"src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc\"\n   {[\"inject-last-value\" \"update\"] 1\n    [\"make-expander\" :map] 1}\n   ;; the content source descriptor: `:store` names a dao.jing byte-store\n   ;; handle the linker interpreter serves content from, never a VM store\n   \"src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/pair.cljc\"\n   {[\"composition\" :map] 1\n    [\"answer\" :map] 1}\n   ;; the link-time stage's context, whose `:store` is that same byte-store\n   ;; handle\n   \"src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/interpreter.cljc\"\n   {[\"dht-context\" :map] 1}\n   ;; the stage load's context: `:store` is the load's content handle\n   \"src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc\"\n   {[\"stage-walk\" \"assoc\"] 1}\n   ;; the handoff demo: shipped definitions, checked\n   \"src/cljc/datomworld/demo/continuation_handoff.cljc\"\n   {[\"datoms->semantic-vm\" \"update\"] 1}\n   ;; a JVM demo moving one VM's whole store to another\nshadow-cljs.edn:19:                 :output-to \"target/node-tests.js\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:71:(defn assoc-module\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:227:(defn register-host-module\nbb.edn:409:  test:sub {:doc \"Subsystem tests, three lanes in parallel: bb test:sub <subsystem>... [--slow]\"\nbb.edn:419: