.vm.linker.publish :as publish]))\n\n\n(defn- class-map?\n  \"The autoload class map entry of a unit set, `{:name \\\"$classmap\\\" :classes\n   {class path} :paths [path ...]}`: it imports every path it lists, so it\n   publishes last and an unpublished path is refused by name (plan P6 R9).\"\n  [unit]\n  (= lower/classmap-name (:name unit)))\n\n\n(defn- config\n  [publish-fn]\n  {:imported-names (fn [{:keys [name packet], :as unit}]\n                     (if (class-map? unit)\n                       (lower/classmap-paths unit)\n                       (lower/imported-unit-names packet {:path name}))),\n   :module-spec (fn [registry {:keys [name packet strict-types], :as unit} php-address deps]\n                  (if (class-map? unit)\n                    (lower/classmap-spec registry unit php-address deps)\n                    (lower/module-spec registry packet\n                                       {:path name,\n                                        :strict-types strict-types,\n                                        :php-address php-address,\n                                        :deps deps}))),\n   :scc-module-specs lower/scc-module-specs,\n   :site-set siteset/publish!,\n   :alias-site-set true,\n   :binder-table nil,\n   :refusal-ns \"yang.php\",\n   :publish-fn publish-fn})\n\n\n(defn publication-order\n  \"Dependency-first names, retaining the input order for acyclic units.\n   Cyclic components appear as sorted member vectors in :order, one\n   publication step per SCC. An include edge no unit and no `published`\n   entry supplies is refused `:yang.php/unpublished-import` by name.\"\n  [units published]\n  (core/publication-order (config publish/publish-module!) units published))\n\n\n(defn publish-units!\n  \"Publish `units` into the content store `handle` over the host registry\n   `registry`, `php` pinned at `php-address` and each include outside the\n   set at its address in `published` ({path address}), in\n   `publication-order`. Answers `{:status :ok :addresses {path address}\n   :bundles {bundle-symbol address}}`, or the first refusal:\n   `publication-order`'s before anything is written, else\n   `publish-module!`'s with a string `:unit`.\"\n  ([handle registry php-address units]\n   (publish-units! handle registry php-address units {}))\n  ([handle registry php-address units published]\n   (publish-units! handle registry php-address units published publish/publish-module!))\n  ([handle registry php-address units published publish-fn]\n   (core/publish-units! (config publish-fn) handle registry php-address units published)))\n9:- BLAKE3-256, the default minting algorithm; and\n10:- SHA-256, an equally supported algorithm available through explicit minting.\n18:A DaoJing store may ordinarily contain addresses produced by both algorithms. Parsing, verification, remote transfer, DHT storage, and cache hydration dispatch according to the algorithm carried by each address.\n49:- BLAKE3 is the default for implicit minting; SHA-256 minting is explicit and ordinary.\n50:- Address verification always uses the algorithm carried by the address, never the current minting default.\n51:- Verification is address-directed; minting primitives are not validators.\n94:The default controls implicit minting only. It is never consulted to verify an existing address.\n246:Malformed, unsupported, noncanonical, or mismatched addresses return false. Canonical-encoder refusal also returns false, making `segment-matches?` a total verification predicate. Minting operations such as `materialize!` and `segment-key` continue to throw on encoder refusal.\n274:When copying content already named by an address into another store, derive the minting algorithm from that address:\n324:- BLAKE3 becomes the one-argument minting default immediately.\n325:- Explicit SHA-256 minting is available immediately as an ordinary registry operation.\n339:2. minting new content;\n343:The classification is a maintained design artifact. Minting and verification must not be conflated.\n348:DaoJing's minting default. They have their own encoders and contracts.\n353:encoders remain independent of DaoJing's addressing encoder and minting\n397:- `dao.jing.remote.step` verification of a `:present` response\n399:- `dao.jing.dht` peer-fetched content verification before caching\n401:- `dao.data.btree.storage` optional fetched-blob verification in the ordinary KV reader\n403:- `yin.vm.content` row fetch verification\n404:- `yin.vm.content` vector fetch verification\n405:- `yin.vm.macro` row-address verification\n406:- `yin.vm.semantic` claimed-segment verification\n407:- `yin.vm.completion` reconstructed-image address verification\n408:- `yin.vm.ledger` row-address verification\n409:- `yin.vm.ledger` output-address verification\n410:- `yin.vm.ledger` record/derivation verification\n413:Backend sites currently compare `segment-hash` with `content-hash`; DaoSpace and yin.vm sites generally re-mint with one-argument `segment-key` and compare whole addresses. Both mechanisms incorrectly substitute minting defaults for address-directed verification.\n426:After verifying the payload, local cache paths materialize using the address-carried algorithm:\n507:- BLAKE3 as the minting default;\n508:- explicit SHA-256 minting;\n509:- algorithm-directed verification; and\n572:- explicit `{:algorithm :sha256}` minting works across public minting APIs (with `request-materialize` and `materialize-async-fn` documented as default-only remote helpers);\n575:- all four address-preserving copy sites derive their minting algorithm from the source address (including `store-tree-async` flushes supplying the source address to the remote store);\n583:- the architectural lint/test preventing default-following verification is active;\n587:### H2: multi-algorithm verification and hardening\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
